      "id": 1,
      "text": "Shiro was founded in 1902 when farm families laid out the townsite and a post office opened with Frances Marion Mayfield as postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/shiro-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name was chosen by Mayfield from botanical names in a nursery catalogue, giving Shiro a floral origin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/shiro-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Shiro grew around the Trinity and Brazos Valley Railway, which ran three miles west of Prairie Plains and spurred a Methodist church and school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/shiro-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1904, Reid Richards and Lou Norman opened a general store that became the town’s first commercial center.",
